# Expectation record for 01_integer_conversions/006_char_signedness_explicit.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 006_char_signedness_explicit
area               = 01_integer_conversions
description        = Character-type conversions using explicit signed char and unsigned char only, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every conversion is explicit and every arithmetic operation is performed on operands
already promoted to int, so no signed type overflows: 255+1 is 256 and -128-1 is -129,
both representable in int. Narrowing to unsigned char is modular and therefore defined;
narrowing to signed char is implementation-defined, never undefined. The only shift count
is the literal 1, strictly less than the 32-bit width of the promoted operand, and no
negative value is ever shifted left. No aliasing violation, no uninitialized read, no
object modified twice between sequence points, at most one side-effecting argument per
call, no dependence on padding bytes.
Every NARROWING is written as an explicit cast, which keeps the strict warning gate clean
without any deviation. The widening conversions the language performs implicitly are still
present and are not being denied -- the integer promotion of each signed char and unsigned char
operand to int, the initialization conversions of the volatile objects, and the default argument
promotions at each printf call -- and every one of them is value-preserving, which is why none
needs a cast and none raises a diagnostic. The _Generic controlling expressions are unevaluated, so they report
promoted types without converting at run time.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This program exists because plain char signedness is implementation-defined and was
measured signed on x86-64 and i686 but unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V 64. Not one object,
parameter or member is declared plain char; only explicit signed char and unsigned char are
used, and the only occurrences of the bare token are inside const char * string pointers
and in explanatory comments. Had a plain char carried any signedness-dependent value here,
cross-backend comparison would report a divergence that says nothing about the compiler.
This difference is handled by construction rather than by an expected-divergence marker,
because a marker implies a test that diverges and this test does not diverge.
Conversion of an out-of-range value to signed char is implementation-defined; all four
supported targets perform two's-complement truncation identically, so uchar_to_schar and
int_to_schar are stable across backends. Arithmetic right shift of a negative value was
likewise measured identical on all four targets.
long and unsigned long are never used, because their width is 4 bytes on i686 and 8 bytes
on the other three targets. No pointer value is printed. No header is included, so -128,
127 and 255 are spelled literally rather than taken from limits.h.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fold_schar_min=-128
fold_schar_max=127
fold_uchar_max=255
fold_schar_to_uchar=255
fold_uchar_to_schar=-56
fold_int_to_schar=-56
fold_int_to_uchar=255
fold_uchar_add=256
fold_schar_sub=-129
fold_schar_shift=-1
fold_uchar_shift=100
fold_schar_uchar_diff=0
run_schar_min=-128
run_schar_max=127
run_uchar_max=255
run_schar_to_uchar=255
run_uchar_to_schar=-56
run_int_to_schar=-56
run_int_to_uchar=255
run_uchar_add=256
run_schar_sub=-129
run_schar_shift=-1
run_uchar_shift=100
run_schar_uchar_diff=0
schar_promotes=int
uchar_promotes=int
END
